\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|    |    |KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: b8-a7, B: b8-c7, B: b8-d6, B: b8-e5, B: b8-f4, B: b8-g3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d4-d5, K: e1-d1, K: e1-d2, K: e1-e2, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as White in what appears to be a mid-game position. Black's king has moved to b7, which is an unusual and somewhat exposed position. Black has castled queenside or their king has wandered. I have a bishop on b8 (deep in Black's territory), which is an advanced piece. Black's kingside appears relatively undeveloped. My king is still on e1 and hasn't castled. I have a central pawn on d4, and there's some pawn tension in the center with Black's e4 pawn.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider several promising moves:\n\n1. **B: b8-a7** - Keeps pressure but doesn't accomplish much tactically\n2. **B: b8-c7** - Attacks the Black king on b7 directly, giving check! This forces Black's king to move\n3. **B: b8-d6** - Centralizes the bishop but loses the attacking tempo\n4. **P: d4-d5** - Advances my central pawn, gaining space\n5. **N: b1-c3** - Develops the knight, attacks the e4 pawn\n6. **K: e1-d2** or **K: e1-e2** - Improves king safety\n\nThe most forcing move is **B: b8-c7**, which gives check to the Black king on b7. This is a powerful tactical shot.\n\nReason: Giving check with B: b8-c7 forces Black's king to move immediately, disrupting their position. The Black king on b7 is already awkwardly placed, and this check will further displace it, potentially creating tactical opportunities. After the king moves (likely Ka8, Ka6, or Kb6), I maintain the initiative and can continue developing with threats. This is far superior to quiet developing moves because it keeps Black on the defensive and exploits the exposed king position.\n\nAnswer: B: b8-c7"}
