ng move. After the Black king moves (likely Kf8, Ke8, or Kg8), my knight will be well-placed on e7, controlling key squares like d5, c6, c8, and f5. This also allows me to potentially coordinate my rooks for an attack. In positions with material advantage, forcing moves that disrupt the opponent's king safety and coordination are typically strongest. The check limits Black's options and gives me the initiative.\n\n**Answer:** N: d5-e7"}
